      "math_academy_id": "q-28515",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+5)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+11x+5$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & 11 & 5 \\\\  &  & -5 & -10 & -5 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+5}={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+5)({x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+5)(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-5$ and a double root at $x=-1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+5)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-5,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251970/assets/430eb562fa8d48f5ff7b25cf7fc83ba0ff84e08de78a87ad9a6e7d5ab22fa14e.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
